<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahavihara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahavihara was the great monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a> in the 3rd century BCE after the arrival of the monk Mahinda. It became the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> orthodoxy, competing with the Abhayagiri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavana">Jetavana</a> monasteries. The commentator Buddhaghosa worked there in the 5th century, and its monastic lineage was later carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p></div>
